# The Root Cause
Tamil title: மூலகாரணம்

"Is this a home? Can one remain here in peace even for a second? What a curse! What a curse! The torment is unbearable. I come home exhausted after work, with every vein and nerve aching. If I try to close my eyes and rest for four seconds, even that is impossible here."

"Why do you agitate yourself unnecessarily? Who told you not to sleep? Tired, you say. Who is not tired? You had not even been lying down for two minutes. I thought you could hardly have fallen asleep already, and so I spoke to you. And why did I speak? To trouble you? I only wanted to ask whether I should make coffee or tea. Why do you flare up at me over that?"

"All right, madam! I was wrong to think that only your father was a lawyer. Enough. Stop your argument."

"Fine. Sleep peacefully. Just say whether you want coffee or tea; that is enough. If, when I ask, you say, 'Make me some, you sour-face! Why did you wake me and bother me?' and then grimace like this, what am I supposed to do?"

"Do not keep talking idly."

"Idle talk and quarrelling are your work, woman, your work. Our lineage does not possess that nature at all, understand?"

"Grandmother from Kodi House has already told me story after story about the greatness of your lineage. You need not tell me again. Please be quiet."

"What are you prattling on about? What do you think you are doing?"

"I am counting the days, O Lord! I am counting how many days remain before the day you take me away. That is what I am counting."

"Kamala! Kamala! Do not provoke my anger! Yes, do not torment me. It will end disastrously. I have returned with a wounded heart. Do not stab that wound and torture me. Do not inflame my rage!"

"You must imagine that my heart is a lotus."

"What grievance does your heart have, madam? Tell me, Goddess Kamalambika, what grievance is gnawing at you now? Last month it was a desire for a screw-pine-flower sari. Only after sixty rupees had been poured out in ritual offering did the possession leave you. The month before that..."

"You bought me a diamond necklace for six thousand. You bought me two elephant calves, and I sent them to my father's house. How much you have poured out! You must be exhausted, poor man. How could I possibly have any grievance?"

"Do you want a necklace for six thousand? You, daughter-in-law of that pauper's household..."

"Go on, speak. Where else will you speak? Who would listen to your raving?"

After this there was no more conversation. Hands began to act, and tears began to fall.

The quarrel between Goddess Kamalambika and her husband Ganapathi Subramaniam escalated into blows. But the cause was not the argument that arose between them at that moment. It was anger already smouldering in both their minds. Even that anger had not arisen from earlier quarrels between the two.

At the office, the manager had given Ganapathi Subramaniam a severe dressing-down because the accounts were wrong. Kamalambika and Kanaka had quarrelled in the precinct of Kannan Temple. The pain from that encounter had kindled Kamalambika's anger.

Ganapathi did not tell Kamala what had happened at the office, and Kamala did not tell her husband about Kanaka's insolent tongue.

Ganapathi returned from work unable to contain his anger, lay down upon the cot, and closed his eyes. In his mind's eye, Manager Mahalingam now stood meekly before him. Ganapathi sat in the director's chair and thundered:

"Mr Mahalingam! Resign from your post without another word. Your brain works less and less with every passing day. Why do you ruin every matter and reduce it to rubble? I try to show you a little respect because you are an older man, but I cannot. I tell you with certainty: if a company has such a foolish manager, it is bound to go bankrupt."

Ganapathi Subramaniam was rebuking him fluently in this fantasy. Could Kamala choose that very moment to ask, "Coffee or tea? Which do you want?" Ganapathi grew angry because she had shattered such a magnificent scene.

A little earlier, Sarada had visited. Kamala had explained Kanaka's insolence to her in exhaustive detail. Sarada heard everything and finally ruled that Kanaka had done nothing wrong. Kamala was furious. She woke her husband intending first to give him coffee and then to narrate her case. It ended in a quarrel.

Ganapathi even forgot the manager's words of condemnation. To Kamala, Kanaka's insolence now seemed ordinary. Both became absorbed instead in the troubles of their married life and worried about those.

"My curse, my fate," Ganapathi muttered in the hall, while Kamala muttered the same in the kitchen.

The following morning, the manager consoled Ganapathi. "This is how family life is, man. Why behave like a madman?" he said.

At the temple tank, Kanaka relieved Kamala's distress. Showing her a scar on her hand, she said, "My husband gave me this stroke with a horsewhip."

The turbulence and anger that erupt within a family, and even the anger and fury that arise in society and between nations, do not necessarily spring from the visible cause present when they burst forth. Careful examination shows that anger produced in the heart at many other times, against many other people, and for many different reasons has collected together and ripened to the point of explosion.

A deafening blast occurs when a spark strikes only because explosive powder has already been packed tightly into a tube. The spark alone did not produce the event.

People live in constant contact with one another through many kinds of relationships. Nations do likewise. The anger that arises repeatedly during those contacts accumulates like explosive powder placed in a tube. Later, some cause resembling a spark appears, and the packed charge explodes.

If the powder had not already been loaded into the tube and compressed, a spark would not produce an explosion. In the same manner, if anger had not gradually gathered in the heart from many causes, a small spark-like incident would not create upheaval within a family.

The pain Ganapathi Subramaniam repeatedly experienced at work, and every grievance that arose there, was explosive powder packed into a tube. Kamala's invitation to drink coffee became the spark.

This is true not only of quarrels between husband and wife. Rivalries and conflicts within societies and between nations are formed in the same way.

Sometimes an explosion causes disaster even before a spark arrives. If too much powder is packed into a tube, mere impact or friction against a hard object may cause an explosion without any spark. The mind has the same nature.

The husband who became angry when Kamala invited him to drink coffee might have become angry even without that cause if the nature and quantity of anger smouldering within him had grown excessive.

Suppose Kamala thought, "Perhaps he is tired or worried. Perhaps some office matter occupies his mind. Whatever the cause, he remains silent. We must not disturb him now," and therefore left her husband sleeping in the reclining chair without waking him. Do you imagine that he would praise her good sense? That too cannot be guaranteed. He might make even her silence the pretext for a quarrel.

"Kamalaambika!" the husband calls harshly.

Kamala already feels distress and anger that her husband possesses a temperament of needless harshness and causeless rage. At the same moment the stove plays tricks, sending out cloud after cloud of smoke.

Irritated, Kamala fires back a sharp "What?" and adds two more arrows: "I am here. I am not dead."

The husband's anger bursts into flame. Kamala comes and stands before him.

"Kamala! Why are you so swollen with pride? A man comes home exhausted like a dog. Is there even a drop of affection in your heart that says you should offer him a mouthful of coffee? You sit there thinking, 'Let him come if he comes; let him die of a broken heart if he dies!'"

Ganapathi Subramaniam hurls these verbal bombs at the graceful woman struck by this unexpected disaster. Unable to endure the words, she covers her ears, her eyes filling with tears, and mutters either "Rama, Rama" or "Siva, Siva."

"Are you calling Rama? Rama!" he shouts, and his hand lands upon Kamala's cheek.

Things can happen this way too. He becomes angry if she calls him to drink coffee, and angry if she does not call him.

Someone hearing only the incident may say, "What a strange man this Ganapathi is!" But is this tendency confined to the imaginary Ganapathi alone? You and I have become Ganapathis many times with our own Kamalas.

14 January 1949
